1) Information / entropy tracking engine

Right now you talk about residue, mismatch, admissibility, but you don’t measure them in a model-independent way.

Add:

Shannon entropy
mutual information between regions
compression complexity (LZ or similar)

Why this matters:

lets you test: “is structure actually forming or just appearing visually?”
bridges all engines with one shared metric
{
  "tool": "info_metrics_module",
  "applies_to": "ALL engines",
  "outputs": ["entropy", "mutual_information", "complexity_score"],
  "purpose": "objective measure of structure vs noise"
}
2) Bifurcation / continuation analyzer (dynamical systems)

You already observe regime changes (SS2 → SS3 → R2), but you’re not formally mapping them.

Add:

parameter continuation (slowly vary K, λ, κ, etc.)
detect fixed points, limit cycles, instability onsets

Why this matters:

turns “it looks like a transition” into mathematically located boundaries
{
  "tool": "bifurcation_analyzer",
  "inputs": ["parameter_range", "observable"],
  "outputs": ["critical_points", "stability_class"],
  "purpose": "formal regime boundary detection"
}
3) Spectral / FFT analysis layer

This is a big gap given your direction (phase, mismatch, packets).

Add:

FFT on spatial fields (PDE, CA, RD)
FFT on time signals (Kuramoto, stochastic, accelerator diagnostics)

Why this matters:

reveals hidden structure (modes, harmonics, coherence bands)
directly connects to your “phase packet” idea
{
  "tool": "spectral_analysis",
  "outputs": ["power_spectrum", "dominant_modes", "coherence_bandwidth"],
  "purpose": "detect hidden phase structure"
}
4) Constraint / optimization solver

You simulate forward—but sometimes you want the inverse:

“What parameters produce corridor formation?”

Add:

simple gradient-free optimizer (CMA-ES, random search, Bayesian opt)

Why this matters:

lets the system discover regimes instead of you guessing them
{
  "tool": "parameter_optimizer",
  "goal": "find configs that maximize target metric",
  "targets": ["coherence", "event_rate", "structure"],
  "purpose": "inverse problem solving"
}
5) Topological data analysis (TDA)

You already talk about “pockets,” “corridors,” “domains.”

Right now that’s visual or heuristic.

Add:

connected components
persistence homology (birth/death of structures)

Why this matters:

gives you a quantitative definition of topology change
{
  "tool": "tda_module",
  "outputs": ["connected_components", "persistence_diagram"],
  "purpose": "measure topology evolution objectively"
}
6) Unit test / falsification harness (this is critical)

You have many sims—but no explicit failure detection system.

Add:

known expected behaviors
“this should NOT happen” conditions

Why this matters:

stops you from accidentally confirming your own framework
{
  "tool": "falsification_suite",
  "tests": [
    "no coupling → no synchronization",
    "high noise → no stable structure",
    "zero mismatch → no evolution"
  ],
  "purpose": "prevent self-confirmation bias"
}
